<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=rifling">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Weapons and war</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rifling</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1520">1520</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rifling is the helical grooving cut inside a gun barrel to spin the projectile, steadying its flight and making it more accurate. Its inventor is not known: straight 'soot grooves' were cut in small arms from at least 1480, and among the earliest recorded spiral-grooved barrels are those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> gunsmith Gaspard Kollner in 1498 and of Augustus Kotter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> in 1520. Because the grooves had to be engraved by hand, rifled guns were at first used mostly by wealthy hunters, and they became common only in the mid-19th century; the first practical military rifled weapons were breech-loaders such as the Queen Anne pistol.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rifling.jpg" alt="Rifling" width="330" height="330" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: baku13 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:105mm_tank_gun_Rifling.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">Muskets</a> are smoothbore weapons that fire ball-shaped ammunition at relatively low velocity. Because of the cost and difficulty of precision manufacture and the need to load quickly from the muzzle, the balls were generally a loose fit, so they bounced off the sides of the barrel and their destination was unpredictable. Where accuracy mattered, as in hunting, a tighter combination of a ball nearer the bore size and a patch was used. The main inspiration for rifling came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> and crossbowmen, who found that projectiles flew faster and more accurately when twisted fletchings made them rotate.</p><p>The inventor of rifling is not definitively known. Straight grooving was applied to small arms from at least 1480, originally as soot grooves to collect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> residue. Among the earliest recorded European attempts at spiral-grooved barrels are those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> gunsmith Gaspard Kollner in 1498 and of Augustus Kotter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> in 1520. Some scholars allege that Kollner's works used only straight grooves until he received help from Kotter.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Rifling is the helical grooving machined into the inside of a firearm's barrel to spin the projectile and improve its stability and accuracy. The ridges left between the grooves are called lands. Its strength is measured by the twist rate, the distance the rifling takes to complete one revolution, and the longer and narrower a projectile is, the faster the twist it needs. In 1879 George Greenhill, a professor of mathematics at the Royal Military Academy at Woolwich, devised a rule of thumb for the best twist, still known as the Greenhill formula.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>True rifling dates from the 16th century, but it had to be engraved by hand and did not become commonplace until the mid-19th century. Early rifled firearms were used mainly by wealthy recreational hunters, who did not need rapid fire and valued accuracy. Military users disliked them because they were hard to clean and hard to load: a bullet that fitted the rifling had to be forced down the bore with a mallet, and a smaller one did not engage the grooves. The first practical military weapons to use rifling with black powder were breech-loaders such as the Queen Anne pistol.</p><p>Patches helped transfer spin to the ball until the hollow-based Minié ball, which expands to seal the bore and engage the rifling. Gain-twist rifling, in which the twist increases towards the muzzle, was used before and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>, including in Colt revolvers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rifling” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1520</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Nuremberg</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Augustus Kotter · Gaspard Kollner</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Gaspard Kollner of Vienna made spiral-grooved barrels as early as 1498 by some accounts, but some scholars hold that his grooves were straight until Kotter helped him.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/arquebus">Arquebus<span>1411</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=rifling">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
